__pycache__/
*.pyc
build/
build_*/
*.o
*.so
*.report
*.ncu-rep
.cache/
compile_commands.json

# built extension and setuptools scratch
quactlize/_C*.so
build/
*.egg-info/

# GENERATED CHECK OUTPUT, not fixtures. gen_moe_units_check.sh does `OUT="$HERE/.moe_units_check${BAD:+_bad}"` and
# mkdir -p's it; the _bad variant is just where the negative-control run writes. 1473 of these files were committed
# by the migration's `cp -r fold_derivation/`, and the copies still carried gen.cmake files naming an absolute path
# in the PRE-migration tree. They also broke `git switch` for anyone who had run the check themselves: git refuses
# to overwrite untracked files with tracked ones, so a checkout aborted listing hundreds of generated units.
dev/fold_derivation/.moe_units_check/
dev/fold_derivation/.moe_units_check_bad/

# COMPILED PROBE BINARIES. The derivation harnesses build as `nvcc l19_multislice.cu -o l19` and
# `g++ ft_check.cpp -o ftchk` (dev/fold_derivation/README.md), so their outputs are EXTENSIONLESS while every
# source carries an extension. 63 of them were committed -- 166 MB, which was essentially the entire 161 MB .git
# -- and because a merge or rebase of that history makes every blob permanently reachable, they also decided how
# this repo can reach main at all (only a squash carries just the final tree).
#
# Ignore-everything-then-un-ignore-anything-with-a-dot is what expresses "no extension" in gitignore, which has
# no such pattern. The trailing `*/` re-admits stub_inc/, syntax_baseline/ and gen_stub/, which are inputs.
dev/fold_derivation/*
!dev/fold_derivation/*.*
!dev/fold_derivation/*/
#
# dev/low_bit/ is NOT repeated here: it already has its own .gitignore naming w2a16_swzl_probe. That file was
# committed anyway, which is the lesson worth keeping -- .gitignore does not touch what is already TRACKED, so
# the rule alone never stopped it and only `git rm --cached` does.
